  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 358 AM CST Mon Nov 2 2026\n\nA deep upper level trough is crossing the Northern and Central \nRockies early this morning, with a series of lee surface \ncyclones developing from southeast Montana to northeast \nColorado. The southern most surface low will become the \ndominant feature and quickly move northeast into eastern South \nDakota by this afternoon. Intense synoptic scale lift will \nproduce widespread rain across the state this morning, tapering \noff from northwest to southeast this afternoon through tonight. \nHowever, there is an emerging solution supported by recent \ninitializations of the HRRR, RAP, REFS, and even the NBM to \nsome extent that stall the low over southwest Minnesota through\nTuesday, which would keep persistent rainfall over the James \nRiver Valley. Probability-matched mean rainfall from high- \nresolution ensembles is consistent with NBM calibrated QPF, \nwhich places at least 1 inch of rain from south central North \nDakota to the James River Valley, and also in west central \nNorth Dakota, mainly over the Badlands. There are also medium \nto high chances for exceeding 2 inches of rain along and east \nof a line from around Linton to Carrington. Despite a lack of \nbuoyancy, poor mid level lapse rates, and some uncertainty on \nthe strength and location of low to mid level frontogenesis, \nother ensemble tools, such as the ECMWF EFI QPF and NAEFS \nprecipitable water exceeding the climatological maximum values,\nlend further confidence to rain amounts this high. This is \nespecially true if rain continues over the James River Valley \nthrough Tuesday, which could result in some locations receiving\nover 3 inches of rain. The lowest rain amounts are forecast \nalong the Canadian border, where there are still medium chances\nfor exceeding half an inch of rain. \n\nThe tightening of the surface pressure gradient will induce \nwindy conditions today, lasting into Tuesday across southern \nand moreso eastern North Dakota. Model soundings continue to \nshow very poor momentum transfer, but that could be overcome by\nthe strong dynamics of the system. Confidence in a longer \nduration of sustained winds around 30 mph is high enough for a \nWind Advisory in areas south of I-94 and west of the Missouri \nRiver, which is in effect from mid morning through late evening \ntoday. An advisory may be needed east of the river on Tuesday \nif the system does stall over southwest Minnesota, but model \nsoundings continue to show very poor mixing potential.\n\nExpect near-steady or slowly falling temperatures through this \nafternoon, with mid 40s northwest to mid 50s south central and \neast. The low temperature forecast for tonight and high \ntemperature forecast for Tuesday are highly dependent on the \nprogression of the low and associated cloud cover. Forecast \nlows below freezing in western North Dakota tonight are likely \nonly achievable if clouds begin to clear before sunrise Tuesday\nmorning. Forecast highs on Tuesday are in the mid to upper 40s,\nbut these could end up cooler if the slower progression \nscenario verifies not only due to persistent cloud cover, but \nalso due to stronger cold air advection.\n\nEnsembles favor a pseudo omega blocking pattern over broad \nsurface high pressure Wednesday and Thursday that could \ntransition to a progressive northwest flow pattern to end the \nweek. 3 out of 4 global ensemble clusters do pick up on a more \npotent shortwave digging down from Canada on Saturday in the \nform of an Alberta clipper. The NBM maintains near to above \nnormal temperatures from the middle of the week through the \nweekend, with highs in the 40s and 50s and lows in the 20s and \n30s.",
